"Boudewijn Waijers" <kroisos@home.nl> wrote:

[And doesn't jumping in water risk drowning?]

> No, but you should only do it at a "safe" place
> (no monsters nearby, and no places out of sight
> close where new creatures may generate), since it
> involves taking off rustable items and dropping
> items that may get otherwise damaged.

My usual plan, and I do this a _lot_, is to pile
_all_ my equipment but a pick-axe outside a room
with a fountain, convert the fountain to pools, take
the pick-axe back to the stash, drop it, pick up
only what I want to dilute or wash clean, go jump in
pools a few times (it can take several), then take
the newly readied loot back and reacquire my loot,
re-dress, re-wield, re-put-on, re-quiver.

This is pretty safe done at a dungeon upper level
and with all known monsters on the level dead, since
it takes many fewer turns than the average "one
monster spawned somewhere on the level" time to
accomplish.  If in addition the room has lockable
doors, and you have a pet you can close outside, all
the better, because the pet can handle monster
generated remotely, and as long as you can see,
there shouldn't be any generated with you in the
room; though you'll then have to carry a key or
lockpick as well, whose rusted state probably
doesn't matter much.

> If you jump into water, *next to the shore*, you
> will crawl out again.  You will drown if you (for
> example) take off a ring of levitation above a wet
> spot surrounded by water.

I seem to recall that you can still drown if you
jump in carrying more stuff than you are strong
enough to crawl out carrying, but this may be only
if it is stuff worn (stuff carried getting dropped
to the bottom of the pool), or only if the PC is
burdened, or totally a mis-remembering, too.

